with each new Pokemon game comes a new  set of Pokemon mechanics and a region to  discover and sword and shield or no  exception the vibrant gallery region is  a consistent delight to explore  incentivizing and rewarding different  types of players in equal measure and  grandiose battles add an exciting  dimension to the familiar gym formula  for an engaging adventure beginning to  end but most notably sword and shield  cut down on the tedious and protracted  elements bogging the series down in  favor of amplifying what makes it great  in the first place this is the most  balanced a Pokemon game has felt in a  long time and with that sword and shield  marked the best new generation of  Pokemon games in years  the game wastes no time in getting you a  starter Pokemon and off on your way to  becoming the champion you can reach the  new wild area an open expanse filled  with all kinds of Pokemon of all levels  within an hour or so of starting your  adventure and the wild area is the  show-stopping feature of this generation  pokemon roam the fields and lakes  changing the weather each day it's all  too easy to set out for one destination  only to be distracted by a Pokemon you  haven't caught yet an item glittering on  the ground in the distance or even an  evolved form of a pokemon that you  didn't realize you could catch in the  wild there's constantly something new to  do or discover and it's there to engage  you right out of the gate random  encounters are no more and instead you  see pokemon roaming Olive Gallery which  helps make each area feel lively and  distinct but there are some Pokemon that  remain hidden in the tall grass denoted  by an exclamation point you have to run  towards the wrestling grass to actually  initiate the fights you're never totally  caught by surprise some Pokemon can only  be found this way - this further  encourages you to fully explore each  locale while making return trips  painless free of constant interruptions  by wild Pokemon or stopping to use  repels for wild Pokemon battles are true  to the formula but for big battles sword  and shield strip out mega evolution and  Z moves in favour of a new battle  mechanic Dyna maxing which is sort of a  combination of the two and can only be  activated in certain locations a Dynomax  Pokemon grows to a massive size and is  stronger overall and its moves convert  to super-powered ones based on type it's  much more bombastic than mega evolution  or even Z moves but functionally it's  simpler and that's refreshing after  years of using both mega evolution and Z  moves and high-level battles Dyna maxing  is a welcome reset that also feels like  a natural evolution of the increasingly  high-octane battle mechanics of recent  games any pokémon can Dynomax - you're  just limited by location rather than an  item or a specific Pokemon so it's a  more flexible way to battle that works  for easygoing and competitive players  alike dynamaxx saying is a fixture of  the new max rates in which you and three  other people or NPCs take on a giant  Pokemon at certain locations in the wild  area raid Pokemon can vary from  run-of-the-mill easy to catch Pokemon to  ones that are incredibly hard to find in  the wild but regardless the  wards are fantastic completing a raid  even if the Pokemon escapes and you fail  to catch it Nets you tons of rare and  important items plus the Pokemon you get  from braids are guaranteed to have some  perfect stats so even duplicate Pokemon  are worth catching again it's a great  alternative to traditional competitive  play after you've beaten the game and  while it does feed into competitive  battling in both the item rewards and  the caliber of Pokemon you're catching  it's satisfying just to overcome the  challenge with friends new Pokemon are  fantastic - quite a few of them seem  geared for competitive play with moves  and abilities that inspire interesting  strategies but there are also the  aesthetically inclined Pokemon like the  incredibly gothcorp of night or the  adorable electric Corgi yam / to inspire  collectors throughout my journey I was  consistently delighted to discover each  new gen 8 pokémon and the gallery'  informs of older ones the Pokedex also  features a healthy mix of old pokemon  from each previous generation there are  certainly surprising omissions but like  with the new pokemon the list includes  both fun pokemon and competitive ones  plus an even spread of types sorting  shield might not have every pokemon in  existence but what's here is balanced  exquisitely for all types of players and  because there are items that give  Pokemon experience points now and  because you can access your Pokemon  boxes almost anywhere you can easily  change up your team on the fly without  having to stop and grind just to get a  new pokemon caught up in level I  experimented with different Pokemon more  during shields main story than I ever  did in a previous Pokemon game and it  made me appreciate the Genet Pokemon  even more small but significant quality  of life tweaks extend the competitive  side of the game to greatly reducing the  remaining barriers to entry there are  now items that allow you to change a  pokemons nature which was the final  missing piece and getting pokemon battle  ready without hours and hours of tedious  breeding and soft resetting in  collecting battling and exploring sword  and shield cut out the bloat and focus  on what makes these pillars of the  Pokemon games so captivating in the  first place there's definitely a lot of  challenge to be had but you're not held  back by overly complicated back-end  systems or hoops to jump through  from the outset you can start wandering  the gallery region seeing its new  pokemon and trying out its new battle  strategies with very little in your way  this leaves you free to enjoy what  pokemon is all about and that makes for  an incredibly strong showing for the  main series debut  on switch  [Music]  you
 

